1. Introduction

Packaging plays a critical role in product safety, cost optimization, and environmental sustainability. Traditional packaging decisions are often heuristic-based and do not systematically evaluate sustainability metrics such as carbon footprint or recyclability.

EcoPackAI addresses this challenge by leveraging machine learning to recommend optimal packaging materials based on product requirements, cost efficiency, and environmental impact.



2. Problem Statement

Organizations lack an intelligent system that:

Evaluates multiple packaging materials objectively

Balances cost, strength, and sustainability

Provides scalable, data-driven recommendations



3. Solution Overview

EcoPackAI uses:

Structured datasets for products and materials

Machine learning models for prediction

AI-based ranking to recommend best materials



4. Module 1 – Data Collection & Database Setup

Created two datasets:

Materials dataset (120 materials)

Products dataset (175 products)

Attributes sourced from industry-realistic ranges

Data stored in PostgreSQL

Ensured schema integrity and data normalization



5. Module 2 – Data Cleaning & Feature Engineering

Checked for missing values and duplicates

Standardized numerical features

Engineered features such as:

Cost score

CO₂ score

Sustainability score

Material suitability score

Prepared clean datasets for ML usage



6. Module 3 – Machine Learning Dataset Preparation

Cross-joined products and materials

Generated ~21,000 product-material combinations

Created target variables:

Target Cost (INR)

Target CO₂ Impact (kg)

Selected relevant ML features

Split data into training (80%) and testing (20%)

Applied feature scaling using StandardScaler



7. Module 4 – AI Recommendation Models
7.1 Cost Prediction Model

Algorithm: Random Forest Regressor

Achieved high accuracy with R² ≈ 0.999

7.2 CO₂ Prediction Model

Algorithm: XGBoost Regressor

Achieved near-perfect prediction with R² ≈ 1.0

7.3 AI Ranking System

Normalized predicted cost and CO₂ values

Combined with sustainability metrics

Generated ranked list of optimal materials per product



8. Module 5 – Flask Backend API

This module focuses on building the backend service that connects machine learning models, the database, and the user interface.

A Flask-based REST API was developed to handle product inputs and return AI-driven material recommendations. The backend performs the following steps:

Accepts product input via JSON request

Retrieves product details from PostgreSQL

Filters eligible materials based on constraints

Applies feature engineering logic

Uses trained ML models to predict:

Packaging cost

CO₂ footprint

Ranks materials using an AI suitability score

Returns structured JSON responses to the frontend

The backend ensures modularity, scalability, and real-time inference capability.

9. Module 6 – Frontend UI Development

This module implements a user-friendly interface for interacting with EcoPackAI.

The frontend was developed using HTML, CSS, and Bootstrap and communicates with the Flask backend via REST APIs.

Key features include:

Product input form with validations

Clean layout using Bootstrap cards

Dynamic results table

Display of ranked material recommendations

Comparison metrics for informed decision-making

The UI demonstrates how AI recommendations can be consumed by non-technical users in a real-world scenario.

10. Conclusion 

EcoPackAI successfully delivers an end-to-end AI system—from data ingestion and machine learning to API-driven inference and user interface presentation.
The project demonstrates strong potential for practical adoption in sustainable packaging decision-making.